Use the vertices' alpha where a colour combiner reads shade alpha

The RDP's colour combiner can take the shade alpha, the alpha that the vertices interpolate, as a colour input
(G_CCMUX_SHADE_ALPHA, 11), as it can take the primitive or environment colour's alpha. Fast3D's combiner knew the other
two but not this one: it printed "Unsupported ccmux: 11" and used 0 there.

WCW/nWo Revenge lights its wrestlers, ropes, turnbuckles and other parts on the CPU: it writes each vertex's brightness
into the vertex's alpha (func_80001930) and draws them with (TEXEL0 - 0) * SHADE_ALPHA + 0 as the colour (combine mode
FC15FE2B FFFFF3F9). With the input at 0, all of them came out black.

The input now gets a slot like the others, and its value at each vertex is the vertex colour's alpha in all four
channels, as PRIMITIVE_ALPHA and ENV_ALPHA are made from their colours.

diff --git a/src/fast/interpreter.cpp b/src/fast/interpreter.cpp
--- a/src/fast/interpreter.cpp
+++ b/src/fast/interpreter.cpp
@@ -343,6 +343,7 @@
                     case G_CCMUX_PRIMITIVE_ALPHA:
                     case G_CCMUX_PRIM_LOD_FRAC:
                     case G_CCMUX_SHADE:
+                    case G_CCMUX_SHADE_ALPHA:
                     case G_CCMUX_ENVIRONMENT:
                     case G_CCMUX_ENV_ALPHA:
                     case G_CCMUX_LOD_FRACTION:
@@ -2306,6 +2307,11 @@
                     case G_CCMUX_SHADE:
                         color = &v_arr[i]->color;
                         break;
+                    case G_CCMUX_SHADE_ALPHA: {
+                        tmp.r = tmp.g = tmp.b = tmp.a = v_arr[i]->color.a;
+                        color = &tmp;
+                        break;
+                    }
                     case G_CCMUX_ENVIRONMENT:
                         color = &mRdp->env_color;
                         break;
